<BaseLayout title="Editorial and Safety Policy" description="How Tension handles sourcing, lived experience, safety claims, corrections, and commercial content.">
  <article class="max-w-2xl mx-auto px-6 py-20 prose">
    <h1>Editorial and Safety Policy</h1>
    <p><em>Last updated: August 2026</em></p>
    <h2>Three kinds of information</h2>
    <p>Articles distinguish between published research, established community practice, and the author's own experience. These are not interchangeable. When evidence is preliminary or contested, the language should say so.</p>
    <h2>Safety-sensitive material</h2>
    <p>Content about consent, altered states, restraint, injury, trauma, mental health, or physical symptoms receives a higher standard of review. It must avoid guarantees, avoid diagnosing readers, and make clear when professional help may be appropriate.</p>
    <h2>Sources</h2>
    <p>Research-based claims link to the original paper, an indexed abstract, or a recognized professional source where possible. Practical advice may also cite established community organizations. Sources are not decoration: they must support the specific claim being made.</p>
    <h2>Commercial independence</h2>
    <p>Tension sells digital resources. Product links and commercial recommendations should be clearly identifiable. Editorial articles are not paid testimonials, and any future affiliate relationship will be disclosed near the relevant link.</p>
    <h2>Corrections</h2>
    <p>Send factual or safety concerns to <a href="mailto:hello@tension.app">hello@tension.app</a>. Material corrections will update the article's modified date and, where useful, include an editor's note.</p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
